{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"9db69734-27d6-449f-b724-8c52fdc8fb40","title":"Definite integral as the area under a curve"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9db69734-27d6-449f-b724-8c52fdc8fb40","step":{"id":"s1","script":"A definite integral measures signed area between a curve and the horizontal axis. Start with a curve that stays above the axis.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Integral as area"},{"op":"figure","id":"graph","region":"right","frame":{"x":[0,2.3],"y":[0,4.8],"axes":"edge","x_label":"x","y_label":"y"},"marks":[{"kind":"fn","id":"curve","y":"x^2","domain":[0,2.15],"label":"y = x²"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"target","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\int_0^2 x^2\\,dx"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9db69734-27d6-449f-b724-8c52fdc8fb40","step":{"id":"s2","script":"From zero to two, the integral captures this entire shaded region. Because the curve is nonnegative, signed area equals ordinary area.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"shading","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"shade","under":"curve","from":0,"to":2,"label":"A"},{"kind":"point","id":"endpoint","at":{"on":"curve","x":2}},{"kind":"segment","from":{"at":"endpoint"},"to":[2,0],"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"area_eq","anchor":{"el":"target","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A=\\int_0^2 x^2\\,dx"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9db69734-27d6-449f-b724-8c52fdc8fb40","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Thin rectangles approximate the area. Adding height times width, then shrinking every width toward zero, gives the exact integral.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"rectangles","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0,0],[0,0.25],[0.5,0.25],[0.5,0]],"closed":true,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[0.5,0],[0.5,1],[1,1],[1,0]],"closed":true,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[1,0],[1,2.25],[1.5,2.25],[1.5,0]],"closed":true,"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"polyline","points":[[1.5,0],[1.5,4],[2,4],[2,0]],"closed":true,"stroke":"dashed"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"sum","anchor":{"el":"area_eq","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx=\\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{i=1}^{n}f(x_i)\\Delta x"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"These rectangles use right endpoints. For this increasing curve, is their total area too large or too small?","expected_gist":"Too large: each rectangle lies above the curve over its interval."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"9db69734-27d6-449f-b724-8c52fdc8fb40","step":{"id":"s4","script":"An antiderivative computes the exact area: evaluate at the upper bound and subtract the lower-bound value. Here the area is eight thirds.","ops":[{"op":"equation","id":"answer","anchor":{"el":"sum","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"A=\\left[\\frac{x^3}{3}\\right]_0^2=\\frac83"},{"op":"figure","id":"area_value","on":"graph","marks":[{"kind":"text","at":[1.55,0.65],"text":"A = 8/3"}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
